<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Zakopane</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1918 CE · capital Zakopane</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A local Polish government in the Tatra resort of Zakopane, headed by the novelist Stefan Zeromski in the first days of independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In late October 1918, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> disintegrated, the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakopane">Zakopane</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podhale">Podhale</a> highlands took over local power from Austrian officials. A National Organisation was formed, and on 13 November the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-zeromski">Stefan Zeromski</a>, who was living in the town, became chairman of its council.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The so-called republic was a temporary administration rather than a separate state: it declared loyalty to a reborn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, took control of the town's offices and police, and organised volunteers to guard the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. It also had to deal with food shortages in the mountain region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Within weeks the new Polish government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> extended its authority to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podhale">Podhale</a>, and by the end of 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakopane">Zakopane</a> was simply part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-zakopane.jpg" alt="Republic of Zakopane" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Arvedui89 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Poland_(1919%E2%80%931928).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zakopane</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stefan Zeromski</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zakopane Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=republic-of-zakopane" aria-label="Republic of Zakopane on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5152 375 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Zakopane lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5472 631L5473 630"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5473 630h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5472 631h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5472 631h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=republic-of-zakopane">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
